1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,530
Welcome back. This lecture is why machines need controllers.

2
00:00:04,530 --> 00:00:10,641
By the end, you will be able to explain what a PLC does, and where it sits between the field devices

3
00:00:10,641 --> 00:00:12,180
and the operator.

4
00:00:12,180 --> 00:00:18,665
We will look at relay logic, meet the motor starter bench, and see where PLCs are used across South

5
00:00:18,665 --> 00:00:21,850
African mines, factories and water works.

6
00:00:23,050 --> 00:00:29,131
Walk onto the floor of almost any mine, factory or water works in South Africa, and you will find

7
00:00:29,131 --> 00:00:33,580
machines that start, stop and protect themselves automatically.

8
00:00:33,580 --> 00:00:39,793
Decades ago, that automatic behaviour came from relay logic. Rooms full of wired relays,

9
00:00:39,793 --> 00:00:42,270
each one doing a single job.

10
00:00:42,270 --> 00:00:48,660
Relay panels work, but they are slow to change. To alter one step, someone has to rewire it.

11
00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:55,668
Today, almost all of that relay logic has moved into a single small computer, the programmable logic

12
00:00:55,668 --> 00:00:57,500
controller, or PLC.

13
00:00:57,500 --> 00:01:03,949
One PLC can replace a whole relay room, and its logic can be changed in software, not with a

14
00:01:03,949 --> 00:01:05,290
screwdriver.

15
00:01:06,490 --> 00:01:09,100
So what does a PLC actually do.

16
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:12,550
It sits between the field devices and the operator.

17
00:01:12,550 --> 00:01:19,190
On one side, field devices. Push buttons, limit switches, sensors. These send signals in.

18
00:01:19,190 --> 00:01:22,440
The PLC reads those signals as inputs.

19
00:01:22,440 --> 00:01:29,413
Inside the PLC, a program decides what should happen. This is the C P U, the central processing

20
00:01:29,413 --> 00:01:30,280
unit.

21
00:01:30,280 --> 00:01:36,650
On the other side, the PLC writes outputs. It switches contactors, lamps and valves.

22
00:01:36,650 --> 00:01:42,237
The operator watches and controls all of this from a panel or a screen, without ever touching the

23
00:01:42,237 --> 00:01:43,140
wiring.

24
00:01:44,340 --> 00:01:49,090
Let's meet the machine we will use throughout Stage One, the motor starter bench.

25
00:01:49,090 --> 00:01:54,450
Here is the Start button and the Stop button, the field devices an operator presses.

26
00:01:54,450 --> 00:01:58,810
Here is the contactor, the switch that actually turns the motor on.

27
00:01:58,810 --> 00:02:02,530
And here is the motor itself, the machine doing the work.

28
00:02:02,530 --> 00:02:08,636
Between the buttons and the motor sits the PLC. It reads the buttons as inputs, and drives the

29
00:02:08,636 --> 00:02:10,630
contactor as an output.

30
00:02:10,630 --> 00:02:17,040
Every machine in this course works the same way. Inputs in, logic in the middle, outputs out.

31
00:02:18,240 --> 00:02:23,240
Later in this stage, you will program this bench yourself, in ladder logic.

32
00:02:23,240 --> 00:02:27,370
You will see the same logic on two real controller families.

33
00:02:27,370 --> 00:02:35,008
On a Siemens S seven 1200, inputs and outputs are addressed like I zero point zero, and Q zero point

34
00:02:35,008 --> 00:02:35,890
zero.

35
00:02:35,890 --> 00:02:42,000
On an Allen Bradley CompactLogix, the same signals get named tags, like Start button.

36
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:47,730
Different brands, same idea. Inputs come in, logic runs, outputs go out.

37
00:02:48,930 --> 00:02:56,398
So, a PLC reads inputs from field devices, runs a program, and writes outputs, the same way relay

38
00:02:56,398 --> 00:03:01,210
logic always has, just faster, and far easier to change.

39
00:03:01,210 --> 00:03:07,196
For your lab, go to the motor starter bench, and name every part. Say whether each one is an input

40
00:03:07,196 --> 00:03:08,490
or an output.

41
00:03:08,490 --> 00:03:14,475
Get comfortable with that one idea, inputs and outputs, because every lecture from here builds on

42
00:03:14,475 --> 00:03:15,160
it.

43
00:03:16,360 --> 00:03:17,950
One last thing.

44
00:03:17,950 --> 00:03:24,104
This is simulated training. It builds real skill, but it is not permission to work on live plant.

45
00:03:24,104 --> 00:03:28,410
Always follow your site's rules, and work under supervision.

46
00:03:28,410 --> 00:03:34,650
Up next, module one continues with inside the PLC, C P U, power and I/O.

47
00:03:34,650 --> 00:03:36,390
I will see you there.

